  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Excellent match for the scenario. The design gives Alice a concrete honest partial-conformance claim shape, anchors protocol identity in an app-contract reference instead of local embodiment labels, separates local/live/audit/effect scopes, and requires durable cited objects plus peer-local records so Bob and Carol can verify what is and is not promised.",
    "strengths": [
      "Claim scope plus implemented/provisional/blocked status directly supports honest partial-conformance wording.",
      "Protocol-boundary identity is separated from embodiment labels, branding, and local implementation shortcuts.",
      "Durable citation and peer-local ledger rules materially improve later audit, replay review, and failure interpretation."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "It is still a guide pattern, not a frozen verification standard or final app contract.",
      "Signature carriage, capability-token language, and some witness details remain intentionally unresolved."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "A weak implementation could still market a scoped manifest as broader interoperability proof than the unfinished contract justifies."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What minimum validation rules must Bob apply to reject underspecified manifests?",
      "How should contract-family references express compatibility and supersession across evolving embodiments?",
      "Which durable audit object should be mandatory for each app class when live behavior later needs durable evidence?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  },
